// The declarations the `containers` fixture's sheet leaves its type cells empty for.
//
// The pair with `containers-expanded` is the gate: that workbook writes the same table with
// `string[]` and two `int[]` columns in its own cells, and the two runs must produce the same
// file. So nothing here may say anything about a value that the other workbook does not also
// say. spec/types/set-and-map.md section 9.

/// What one row of the shop carries.
struct Bag
    /// The badges shown on this row - "new", "sale". No two the same, and each is shown to
    /// somebody, so they are gathered for translation.
    ///
    /// **The role goes inside the brackets**, because a container's element constraints are
    /// written on the argument they are about - section 2.2 of the spec.
    field tags   set<string(text)>
    /// What each item costs here, by that item's key.
    field prices map<int,int(min=1)>
    /// What one roll hands out, by the roll's number. The value is a struct, so this map is
    /// a key column beside a group of them.
    field drops  map<int,Reward>

/// One thing a row hands out.
///
/// Here for the map whose value is a struct, which is the shape one cell of pairs cannot
/// hold - `containers-refused` writes that and is told so.
struct Reward
    /// Which item, as that table's key.
    field itemId int
    /// How many of it.
    field count int

/// What falls out of one crate, by the roll that produced it.
struct Crate
    /// The value is a struct, so this map is two columns and not one cell of pairs.
    field drops map<int,Reward>
